  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nIf you've heard or read our discussions recently, the content\nmay seem to be a on repeat in sharing similar weather messages.\nToday is no different; that's because the short and long range\nforecasts have similar outlooks featuring daily \nshower/thunderstorm chances mainly in the afternoon/evening \nhours as weakly developed shortwaves track over the warm, moist\nairmass. The broad concerns focus on high PWATs (hovering\nbetween the 80th to 90th percentiles) and modest buoyancy\n(ranging from 800-1600 J/kg sbCAPE) within a weakly sheared\nenvironment creating non-zero flash flood and damaging wind\nrisks; timing and coverage of these storms/risks are more\nlikely to be influenced by prior day's action than any well-\nmodeled synoptic wave. There are 3 periods to note:\n\nFor Friday through Saturday: Increasing mid-level dry air and \npotential for an upstream shortwave to cross favors more \nwidespread activity for Friday/Saturday while raising the \ndamaging wind risk. Those winds may remain to isolated for an \nSPC outlook but machine- learning weather models suggest \nslightly higher probabilities of such occurrence that previous \ndays.\n\nFor Sunday: though storms can't be completely ruled out,\nensemble guidance is trending toward more \"quiet\" weather for\nthis day due to the potential for brief subsidence in the wake\nof a shortwave suppressing storm activity along with a drop in\ncolumn moisture lowering instability.  \n\nFor Monday/Tuesday: ensemble trends lean towards a more NW flow\npattern while near-sfc return flow recreates the warm, moist\nairmass, returning thunderstorm chances. If more defined shortwave\nmovement becomes clearer during this period, MCS-like structures\nmay be the more likely storm mode vs. diurnal pop-up storms that\nwould create the highest damaging wind risks of the period.\nLarge variability remains in this (and other) solutions, but\nbears monitoring.",
